@reporter By default, OpenVINO will use hyper threading on i3 and i7, but not on Xeon. In your case, hyper threading is enabled on Xeon but not be used.

If you also want to hyper threading on Xeon, please additionally set `ov::inference_num_threads` to the total number of processors on Xeon during `compile_model()`. 

For example `exeNet = core.compile_model(model, "CPU", ov::inference_num_threads(48));`

@participant1 
I have uploaded the app with the model [here](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1ta32_564Buz9sKnRv59QJblp6Ivaf7Bb?usp=sharing)
you can run `DvlEYE.py` that will run `yolov8AividEYE.py` to run the yolov8n model OR you can run `Dockerfile`

@reporter  When hyper threading is enabled on two sockets Xeon platform, OpenVINO only use half processors for inference on throughput mode by default. 

If you want to use all processors in your case, please type below comment
`compiled_model = ie.compile_model(model=model, device_name="CPU", config={"INFERENCE_NUM_THREADS":"16"})`

@participant2 Throughput performance hint expect user user run inference with multiple inputs in parallel. However there is only one input during inference in your sample code. Please refer to AsyncInferQueue part in https://github.com/openvinotoolkit/openvino_notebooks/tree/main/notebooks/115-async-api to perform parallel inference with optimal number of inference requests.

Xeon 6336Y is 24 cores 48 threads CPU. However your system show 2 sockets, 16 cores 16 threads CPU. May I know if you are running in VM or Docker? If yes, could you share what's the real CPU processors on bare metal machine? 

If you are running docker on 8 cores 16 threads CPU (hyperthreading enabled) but docker reports 16 cores 16 threads CPU, OpenVINO runtime will schedule inference for 16 cores 16 threads CPU and will not get full CPU utilization.

@participant2, Xeon might be overkill for nano models. Could you try to reshape the model to process 100 and 1000 images (sample code below)? Also, do not enumerate the input for one image only. Try it using 100 and 1000 images. Then check the latency. The 100 images should be within 1 second as the benchmark_app reporting 150 FPS.

ov_model.reshape([100,3, 416, 416])
ov_model.reshape([1000,3, 416, 416])

@participant4 please try below configuration mentioned in https://github.com/openvinotoolkit/openvino_notebooks/issues/916 
```
setting = {"INFERENCE_NUM_THREADS":"32", "AFFINITY":"NUMA"}
compiled_model = core.compile_model(model=model, device_name="CPU", config=setting)
```

@participant2 May I know if there is any update?
